window.MONSTER_CHUNK_118 = [{"name":"Giant Saw-toothed Beetle","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":18,"ac_mods":"+8 natural","hp":32,"hd":"5d8+10","hdvalue":5,"fort":6,"ref":1,"will":1,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +5 (2d6+3 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":15,"dexterity":11,"constitution":14,"wisdom":10,"charisma":6,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":15,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["cluster (2-5)","colony (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"The most noticeable feature of this beast is its oversized mandibles, jagged and serrated. The beetle's wing covers and carapace are silverygreen and have a dull sheen. Its legs are long and marked with spiraling bands of green and black.","group":"Beetle","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Giant saw-toothed or serrated beetles are deadly predators that hide in their burrows and ambush creatures that come too close. They make their homes in deep burrows on the forest floor and usually cover the opening with sticks, leaves, branches, and whatever else they can find. It takes a successful Spot check (DC 20) to notice a giant saw-toothed beetle's hidden burrow.\n\nThese beetles form colonies of about ten creatures. They are highly aggressive creatures and actively seek sources of meat. Their usual diet consists of small forest animals such as rabbits, deer, or moles. They are even known to devour their own when food is short or when a member of the colony becomes sick or weak.\n\nGiant saw-toothed beetles wait for their prey to come close to their burrow. Hiding at the edge of the burrow, the beetle sits motionless until its target is within range. It then charges out, grabs its target with its serrated mandibles and clamps down, holding on until the prey dies.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Giant Water Beetle","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"vermin","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"watersense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":16,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+0 Dex, +5 natural, +1 size","hp":30,"hd":"4d8+12","hdvalue":4,"fort":4,"ref":0,"will":0,"defensive_abilities":["ink cloud"],"immunities":["mind-affecting effects"],"weaknesses":["water dependent"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10},{"type":"swim","speed":60}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d8+3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":15,"dexterity":13,"constitution":16,"wisdom":10,"charisma":9,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmd":16,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":0,"special":"(+4 underwater) Stealth +1 (+5 underwater) Swim +10"}],"environments":["any freshwater aquatic"],"organizations":["cluster (2-5)","colony (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This giant beetle has a cylindrical and hydrodynamic body that tapers into a pointed tail section. Its wing covers and carapace are brownish-black and its legs are dull yellow. A silver stripe runs along its dorsal side.","group":"Beetle","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Ink Cloud","kind":"Ex","details":"Once per minute as a swift action, a giant water beetle can emit a cloud of jet-black ink that fills a 10-foot cube. The cloud provides total concealment, which the giant water beetle normally uses to escape. All vision within the cloud is obscured."},{"type":"Water Dependent","kind":"Ex","details":"Giant water beetles can survive out of the water for 1 hour per 2 points of Constitution. After that, they begin to suffocate."},{"type":"Watersense","kind":"Ex","details":"A giant water beetle's water sense ability functions exactly like blindsense, except it only functions underwater."}],"description":"These highly aggressive beetles make their home in deep fresh water, such as rivers, lakes, pools, and seas and can be found in any climate, stretching from the warmest to the coldest. Giant water beetles are predators and sustain themselves on fish and other aquatic animals. They spend most of their lives in the water, rarely coming on land. If encountered on land, a giant water beetle will never be more than 30 ft. from any source of water. They are diurnal creatures and hunt during the day, diving with blinding speed when they spot a potential meal in the water. When not actively hunting, they simply drift along with the current. Giant water beetles lair on the bottoms of lakes, pools, and seas. If a colony is found, there will be at least 1 female giant water beetle and 2d4 eggs. Giant water beetle eggs are brownish with flecks of green. Eggs hatch within three weeks after the female deposits them Giant water beetles reach full maturity in six to eight weeks. When hunting, these creatures prefer to attack by ambushing their prey from above. They drift along and then dive at the target with blinding speed (treat this as a charge). Prey is bitten by the giant water beetle's oversized mandibles.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Greater Fire Crab","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","subtypes":["extraplanar","fire"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"tremorsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+7 natural, -1 size","hp":30,"hd":"4d8+12","hdvalue":4,"fort":7,"ref":1,"will":1,"immunities":["fire","vermin traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to cold"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +7 (1d6 +4 plus 1d8 fire plus grab)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d6 plus 1d8 fire"},{"type":"heat"}],"strength":18,"dexterity":11,"constitution":16,"wisdom":11,"charisma":2,"baseatk":3,"cmb":8,"cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Weapon Focus (claw)","type":"bonus"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":0},{"skill":"Swim","value":12}],"environments":["any (Plane of Fire)","any (Plane of Molten Skies)"],"organizations":["solitary","cluster (2-5)","colony (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A massive crab with a square-shaped reddish-brown carapace covered with dark red and yellow markings scuttles forth, pincers raised. Tiny flames lick its body, erupting at irregular intervals from its underbelly.  Its eyes are perched on the end of two large eyestalks that protrude from the center of the carapace.","group":"Fire Crab","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Heat","kind":"Ex","details":"The body of a greater fire crab generates intense heat, dealing 1d8 points of fire damage with its touch. Creatures attacking a fire crab unarmed or with natural weapons take fire damage each time their attacks hit. Any melee hit by a fire crab deals fire damage."}],"description":"Fire crabs are invertebrates found roaming the fiery shores of the Elemental Plane of Fire and the Plane of Molten Skies (see the City of Brass by Necromancer Games).\n\nFire crabs can be found crawling the shores or swimming in pools and lakes of liquid flame. They are generally non-aggressive creatures and spend their time eating the heated rocks and plants found in the abovementioned planes. Food not eaten is carried and stored in their lair, which takes the form of a large burrow under the fiery waters or on the shores near their \"water source.\" Such burrows are sometimes very large and can contain up to 20 of these creatures. On the Material Plane, fire crabs lair in volcanoes and near hot springs.\n\nFire crabs have large claws and in males, one claw is always larger (at least three times larger) than the other. Fire crabs have six segmented and spindly legs, blackish-red in color.\n\nFire crabs are highly territorial and defend their lair with great ferocity.\n\nNormally non-aggressive creatures, if their lair is threatened, they fight to the death. Against weaker prey, fire crabs usually just slash with their claws. Stronger foes are grabbed and squeezed by the fire crab. Once they grab an opponent, they hold on, allowing their fires to engulf the foe.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Gronk","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CN","size":"Large","type":"monstrous humanoid","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+4 natural, -1 size","hp":34,"hd":"4d10+12","hdvalue":4,"fort":6,"ref":4,"will":4,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"gore +7 (1d6+4), 2 slams +7 (1d6+4) or greatclub +7 (2d8+6) and +2 gore (1d6+2)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","strength":19,"dexterity":10,"constitution":16,"intelligence":8,"wisdom":11,"charisma":9,"baseatk":4,"cmb":9,"cmd":19,"feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Intimidate","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Survival","value":11}],"languages":["Giant"],"environments":["cold plains"],"organizations":["solitary","band (2-5)","nomadic pack (6-30)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"The most notable feature of the hairy humanoid is large curved horn projecting from the forehead. The creature's thick limbs and torso betray its muscular power.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Gronks are the typical big, dumb brutes; a male gronk is on average approximately 9 ft. tall, with females only slightly smaller. A gronk has mottled gray skin and shaggy brown hair that is thickest at its head and shoulders and gradually thins out around its waist. The arms and legs of a gronk are massive, like tree limbs attached to its thick barrel-like torso. The face of a gronk seems almost lost amid the long hair hanging from its head. The most notable feature of a gronk's face is the long, rhinoceros-like horn between its eyes. Gronks dwell in small family bands or nomadic packs. They are huntergatherers, wandering the cold plains they call home. Gronk packs never remain in one place for more than a month before moving on to greener pastures and better hunting grounds. They have been known to engage in barter with traders who cross their territories. Of particular interest to gronks are frost giants, with whom they have some sort of kinship or bond (though the extent of such a bond is unknown). Gronks are fiercely territorial, but will not attack strangers who are simply passing through. They know how long it should take to cross their lands, and anyone remaining there for longer than that is subject to a raid. During such raids, gronks often subdue rather than kill their opponents. Subdued opponents are left to fend for themselves in the wild while the gronks carry off their gear and supplies. The only creatures that are immediately attacked when they enter gronk lands are orcs. There is some ancient enmity between the two races that forces a gronk to attack any orc or half-orc on sight. Such creatures are never shown quarter in combat and all battles between a gronk and orc or half-orc ends when one or the other dies. Gronks enjoy combat and enter into melee with unashamed glee. They can fight either armed or unarmed, and actually prefer the latter. Among weapons, gronks tend to use simple weapons such as clubs. They rarely use missile weapons of any kind.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Gutlslug","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"aberration","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"blindsight","modifier":"30 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +3 natural","hp":19,"hd":"3d8+6","hdvalue":3,"fort":3,"ref":5,"will":3,"weaknesses":["gutslug vulnerabilities"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"fly","speed":50,"maneuverability":"perfect"}],"melee":"type +6 (1d6+2 plus grab plus blood drain)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft. (coiled)","special_attacks":[{"type":"blood drain","details":"1d4 Con"},{"type":"sneak attack","details":"2d6"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":19,"constitution":14,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":11,"charisma":3,"baseatk":2,"cmb":4,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Fly","value":12},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-5)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This slimy, sticky, worm bears a striking resemblance to humanoid entrails. Its lengthy body looks like a lumpy, veined, gray tube approximately 10 ft. long, but no thicker than a sword handle. It has no eyes, and its large, round mouth resembles the suckered mouth of a leech.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Blood Drain","kind":"Ex","details":"A gutslug that grapples a foe drains blood. This deals 1d4 points of Constitution drain each round the grapple is maintained. Once a gutslug has drained 8 points, it detaches and flies or slithers away to digest its meal."},{"type":"Vulnerabilities","kind":"Ex","details":"Gutslugs are vulnerable to salt. A pint of salt deals 1d4 points of damage to a gutslug for 1d2 rounds and causes it to lose its racial bonus to grapple checks. A pint of alcohol poured or thrown on a gutslug deals no damage but causes it to lose its racial bonus to grapple checks."}],"description":"These hideous creatures have many names-entrail worms, intestine crawlers, viscera worms, and several others. The most common name assigned to these leech-like parasites is gutslug. Gutslugs are most often found in underground caverns, though some do lair above ground. Aboveground gutslugs build their nests in forested areas, often near lakes or rivers. They are never encountered in warm or cold regions as they detest both extremes. They are nocturnal hunters and generally avoid the sunlight if possible. (Sunlight doesn't damage them, they just don't like it.) Subterranean gutslugs nest in crevasses, or in small caves or natural caverns, sometimes near a source of water. The lair is coated with a thick layer of slimy and sticky mucus (regurgitated by the gutslug). This mucus has a strong acidic stench and is easily detected at ranges of 30 ft. or less. At irregular intervals (especially after feeding), the body of a gutslug widens to accommodate a bag-like stomach. When a warm-blooded living creature is detected, a gutslug flies or crawls directly toward it, attacking and biting almost mindlessly.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Hanged Man","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","init":5,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +4 natural","hp":22,"hd":"4d8+4","hdvalue":4,"fort":2,"ref":2,"will":4,"immunities":["undead traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +5 (1d4+2)","ranged":"rope touch +5 (entangle)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"drag"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":13,"intelligence":9,"wisdom":11,"charisma":13,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmb_special":"+5 vs. drag","cmd":16,"feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (rope)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":9,"special":"+19 with rope"},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8}],"languages":["Common (cannot speak)"],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"half standard","description_visual":"This creature has pale skin pulled tight over its bones. Its head hangs at an odd angle, seemingly unsupported by a broken neck. A rotted noose and several ft. of rope hang from its neck and trail off behind it. Its eyes have no pupils.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Drag","kind":"Ex","details":"If the hanged man entangles a foe with his rope attack, it drags its opponent 10 ft. closer as a standard action on each subsequent round that it succeeds on a CMB check (including a +5 racial bonus) without provoking attacks of opportunity. When an opponent is dragged within 5 ft., the hanged man can attack with both of its claws in the same round. It gains a +4 attack bonus against opponents entangled in its rope."},{"type":"Entangle","kind":"Su","details":"The hanged man's noose is a 20-ft. length of rope that entangles opponents of any size as an animate rope spell (CL 15th, DC 21). A hanged man can lash its rope outward 20 ft. with no range penalty. The rope functions only for the hanged man it belongs to and no other. It has AC 22, hardness 10, hp 10, and DR 5/slashing. If the rope is severed, both it and the hanged man are destroyed. The save DC is Strength-based."}],"description":"A hanged man is the restless corpse of an evil humanoid that was hanged or the spirit of one wrongfully accused of a crime and hanged. It is a malevolent and vengeful creature that attacks living creatures on sight.\n\nHanged men are usually encountered near the area where they were killed or in cemeteries, mausoleums, or graveyards where their body was laid to rest. They prey on the living and are found near civilized areas where their food supply is plentiful. Its lair is either its final resting place or a secluded and overgrown area near the place of its death. Its lair is always scattered with the remains of its victims, including any treasure a former meal happened to be carrying.\n\nA hanged man attacks primarily with its rope, which has become imbued with negative energy and now acts as an extension of the hanged man itself. It uses its rope to capture victims and draw them in where it rips and tears them to shreds with its filthy claws.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Hedon","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"fey","init":6,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":14,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +2 natural","hp":16,"hd":"3d6+6","hdvalue":3,"fort":5,"ref":5,"will":5,"dr":"5/cold iron","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +2 (1d4+1 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rake","details":"2 claws, 1d6+1"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":3,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":5,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"constant","spells":[{"spell":"detect good"},{"spell":"speak with animals"}]},{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"snare"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"entangle"},{"spell":"heat metal"},{"spell":"scare"},{"spell":"shatter"},{"spell":"shocking grasp"}]}],"strength":12,"dexterity":15,"constitution":14,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":14,"charisma":15,"baseatk":1,"cmb":2,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":14,"feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Bluff","value":8},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":8},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Perform (dancing)","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8}],"languages":["Common","Sylvan","speak with animals"],"environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-5)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This creature resembles an ugly elf with the lower torso of a giant bird. Its legs are the clawed talons of a falcon, covered in sickly greenish-brown feathers. Its hands end in wicked claws with dirty brown nails. A forked black tongue dances across the creature's lips, revealing a mouth of sharpened fangs.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","description":"The origins of the hedon are shrouded in mystery but many sages and scholars believe the creatures to be the union of a harpy and an elf or a harpy and some fey creature. Other sages dispute these claims and believe the hedon are the result of a curse laid on an entire community of fey creatures by a vengeful annis or hag.\n\nHedons are malevolent and violent fey creatures that dwell in darkened forests found on the fringes of civilization. They take great delight in tormenting travelers and townsfolk and always lair near heavily traveled roads or paths and communities. Many hedons sneak into nearby towns under the cover of darkness and slaughter livestock or kidnap children (because they are easier to carry and control than adults).\n\nHedons lair in caverns, caves, or nests built from branches, straw, twigs, and so on. Lairs are always kept well hidden away from prying eyes and are difficult to locate by all but the best of scouts and trackers. A typical hedon lair contains 2-4 adults and an equal number of noncombatant young. Mating season for hedons is year round and the female lays a clutch of 1d4 eggs. The eggs hatch after about 3 months and young reach maturity within 2 or 3 years.\n\nA hedon appears as a tan-skinned elf with the lower torso of a hawk or falcon. It rarely wears clothes, but should it choose to do so (and some do, especially to hide their appearance when they attempt to move through a populated area without drawing undue attention to themselves), it usually dons a long hooded cloak or robe of green, gray, or brown. Hedons rarely carry weapons, preferring to fight with their claws.\n\nA hedon attacks with its spell-like abilities, targeting the strongest perceived foes first. It quickly follows this barrage with melee by grabbing its foe and raking with its clawed ft.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Hippocampus","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CG","size":"Large","type":"magical beast","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +4 natural, -1 size","hp":34,"hd":"4d10+12","hdvalue":4,"fort":7,"ref":6,"will":2,"weaknesses":["water dependent"],"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":60}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d4+4) or tail slap +7 (1d6+4) or butt +7 (1d3+4)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":18,"dexterity":15,"constitution":16,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":12,"charisma":10,"baseatk":4,"cmb":9,"cmd":21,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":7,"special":"+11 underwater"},{"skill":"Swim","value":18}],"environments":["temperate aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","herd (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This strange creature appears to be half-horse and half-fish. The front half resembles a sleek stallion with a flowing mane and long, sleek legs ending in wide fins rather than hooves. The hindquarters are that of a great fish.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Water Dependency","kind":"Ex","details":"Though they are unable to move on land, a hippocampus can breathe air (such as if it surfaces from the water). It can survive out of the water for 1 minute per 1 point of Constitution (see to the Drowning)."}],"description":"A hippocampus is often called a merhorse or sea horse, for it is indeed a half-horse/half-fish creature of the sea. The hindquarters of the animal are that of a great fish. Its body is covered in fine scales in the fore parts and large scales elsewhere. The hippocampus' scales vary in color from ivory to deep green, with shades of blue and silver. Aquatic races often tame these animals, and they make fine steeds, for they are strong, swift, and very intelligent. A hippocampus is about 8 ft. long and weighs about 600 pounds. Hippocampuses speak Aquan. Some speak common. Hippocampuses are docile creatures and only fight if threatened or if an ally is threatened. In combat, they use their powerful bite or tail slap. On rare occasions, the hippocampus may butt an opponent with its head. Training a Hippocampus A hippocampus requires training before it can bear a rider in combat. To be trained, a hippocampus must have a friendly attitude toward the trainer (this can be achieved through a successful Diplomacy check). Training a friendly hippocampus requires six weeks of work and a DC 25 Handle Animal check. Riding a hippocampus requires an exotic saddle. A hippocampus can fight while carrying a rider, but the rider cannot also attack unless he or she succeeds on a Ride check. Hippocampus eggs are worth 1,500 gp apiece on the open market, while young are worth 2,500 gp each. Professional trainers (usually tritons) charge 1,000 gp to rear or train a hippocampus. Carrying Capacity: A light load for a hippocampus is up to 300 pounds; a medium load, 301-600 pounds; and a heavy load, 601-900 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Hippocampus originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1977) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Hoar Spirit","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","subtypes":["cold"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"heatsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +5 natural","hp":26,"hd":"4d8+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":3,"ref":3,"will":6,"immunities":["cold","undead traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +7 (1d4+3 plus 1d3 cold plus glaciate)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"cold","details":"1d6"},{"type":"glaciate","details":"DC 14"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":4,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":4,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"cone of cold"}]}],"strength":17,"dexterity":15,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":15,"charisma":15,"baseatk":3,"cmb":6,"cmd":18,"feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (claws)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":6},{"skill":"Climb","value":10},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9}],"environments":["any cold land"],"organizations":["pack (2-5)","gang (6-11)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This being is a gaunt humanoid dressed in tattered rags. Its skin is pale gray and ice crystals have formed on various parts of its body. Its hands end in claws, each with translucent icy blue nails. Its eyes are frozen solid and show no signs of life.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Cold","kind":"Su","details":"Hoar spirits generate intense cold. It deals an extra 1d3 points of cold damage with each claw attack. Creatures attacking a hoar spirit with unarmed or natural attacks take 1d3 points of cold damage each time they hit."},{"type":"Glaciate","kind":"Su","details":"A creature hit by a hoar spirit's claw must succeed on a DC 14 Fortitude save or by paralyzed by bone-numbing cold for 1d4+1 rounds and take 1 point of Dexterity damage. The save DC is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Heatsense","kind":"Ex","details":"Hoar spirits can detect heat (such as that generated by living creatures) within 60 ft., including the heat of invisible creatures. It functions as blindsense in regards to any non-visible heat sources within its range."}],"description":"Believed to be the spirits of humanoids that freeze to death either because of their own mistakes or because of some ritualistic exile into the icy wastes by their culture, hoar spirits haunt the icy wastelands of the world seeking warm-blooded living creatures in which to share their icy hell.\n\nHoar spirits are rarely encountered alone. Though they act independently and never communicate, something draws these creatures to each other.\n\nPerhaps some semblances of their former lives or maybe like-minded creatures that share the same fate are drawn together. Sages have speculated this for ages.\n\nHoar spirits don't speak, and it isn't known if they even understand any language at all. They have a distinctive low-pitched moan that sounds like a whistling wind blowing through ice and snow covered trees.\n\nWhen confronted, a hoar spirit attacks with its claws attempting to freeze its foe. If facing multiple opponents, a hoar spirit blasts them with its cone of cold before engaging them with its claws. Creatures killed by a hoar spirit are dragged to its icy lair, kept on ice, and devoured at the monster's leisure.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Ice Drake","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Small","type":"dragon","subtypes":["cold"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +6 natural, +1 size","hp":22,"hd":"3d12+3","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":4,"will":2,"immunities":["cold","paralysis","sleep"],"sr":18,"weaknesses":["vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"fly","speed":50,"maneuverability":"average"}],"melee":"bite +6 (1d6+1), 2 claws +5 (1d4+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"breath weapon","details":"20 ft. cone, DC 12, 2d8 cold Space 5 ft."},{"type":"Reach 5 ft.","details":"10 ft. with bite"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":7,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":8,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"2/day","spells":[{"spell":"fear"},{"spell":"sleep"}]}],"strength":13,"dexterity":13,"constitution":13,"intelligence":7,"wisdom":8,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":3,"cmd":14,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Flyby Attack"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Bluff","value":6},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":4},{"skill":"Fly","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":5}],"languages":["Draconic"],"sq":[{"type":"alternate form 2/day","details":"form of the dragon I [white dragon only]"}],"environments":["cold hills","cold mountains"],"organizations":["pair","clutch (2-5)","family (6-8)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This slender creature looks like a small immature white dragon with icy white scales and sapphire eyes. A chill hangs in the air around the creature. Its claws are sharpened and have icy blue nails.","group":"Drake","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Alternate Form","kind":"Su","details":"Twice per day, an ice drake can assume the shape of a young white dragon for 2 hours."}],"description":"Ice drakes are found in cold mountainous caves and caverns. Most encounters are with a solitary drake. Only in the winter months is it common to find a mated pair or family. An ice drake's scales are white, and it is often mistaken for a young white dragon. Ice drakes range from 3 ft. to 6 ft. long.\n\nAn ice drake opens combat from the air using its icy breath weapon. If it expends its breath weapon, it relies on its claws and bite to finish off any remaining opponents.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Ice Drake originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Ice Troll","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Large","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["cold","giant"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"90 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":12,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +2 natural, -1 size","hp":18,"hd":"2d8+9","hdvalue":2,"hp_mods":["regeneration 3 (acid or fire)"],"fort":6,"ref":1,"will":2,"dr":"5/magic","immunities":["cold"],"weaknesses":["slashing weapon vulnerability","vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +4 (1d6+4)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rend","details":"2 claws, 1d6+6"}],"strength":19,"dexterity":12,"constitution":16,"intelligence":9,"wisdom":10,"charisma":6,"baseatk":1,"cmb":9,"cmd":23,"feats":[{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":2}],"languages":["Giant"],"environments":["cold mountains"],"organizations":["solitary","band (3-6)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This large, powerful creature has semitransparent flesh of light blue. Its body is completely hairless and its cold, piercing eyes are stark white.","group":"Troll","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Slashing Weapon Vulnerability","kind":"Ex","details":"If an opponent rolls a natural 20 with a slashing weapon against an ice troll (and subsequently confirms the critical hit), the ice troll must succeed on a Fortitude save (DC 10 + damage taken) or lose a limb (roll 1d6: 1-3 arm, 4-6 leg; 50% chance of either right or left). An ice troll that loses a leg falls to the ground, but can continue moving at one-half speed. Severed limbs cannot attack but move at a speed of 30 ft. toward the nearest source of water or ice."},{"type":"Regeneration","kind":"Su","details":"If an ice troll loses a limb or body part, the lost portion grows back in 2d6 minutes so long as both the ice troll and lost body part are within 30 ft. of each other and are both in contact with ice or water. The creature can reattach the severed member instantly by holding it to the stump, but only if the severed member has been in contact with ice or water for at least 1 round."}],"description":"Ice trolls are relatives of normal trolls, but are decidedly more cunning, ruthless, evil, and despicable. They make their homes in very cold climates, always near a pool of water (either natural or troll-made). The strongest (and sometimes most intelligent) member of a band is usually the leader. Unlike their trollish cousins, ice trolls don't rush headlong into battle. They prefer to attack the weakest opponents first if they can. If combat goes against an ice troll, it doesn't hesitate to flee. Ice trolls savor the taste of human flesh and construct their lairs near civilized areas where humans are plentiful. Ice trolls often set traps for humans and either devours them immediately or captures them and carries them back to their lair. Captured humans are caged and fattened up before they are eaten. An ice troll stands about 8 ft. tall and weighs 450 pounds.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Jack-o-lantern","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"plant","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 dodge, +1 natural","hp":33,"hd":"6d8+6","hdvalue":6,"fort":6,"ref":5,"will":2,"immunities":["plant traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"3 slams +6 (1d6+2 plus grab) or pitchfork +6 (1d6+3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"constrict","details":"1d6+2"},{"type":"rend","details":"2 slams, 2d6+3"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":11,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":12,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"fire seeds","details":"first version only on its own seeds, ranged touch +7"}]}],"strength":14,"dexterity":16,"constitution":13,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":11,"charisma":2,"baseatk":4,"cmb":5,"cmd":19,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Mobility"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Stealth)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":6},{"skill":"Stealth","value":16}],"environments":["any land"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is a roughly humanoid shaped tangle of vines and leaves with a large pumpkin for its head. The pumpkin-head bears a leering face that appears to have been carved there, and glows from within with an eldritch fire.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"A jack-o-lantern is an animated plant creature brought to life by a combination of druidic magic and fey sprits. The nature of the face generally reflects the alignment of the animating spirit. A jack-o-lantern attacks with its many tendrils, rending and tearing at its prey. Occasionally one will be armed with a pitchfork or scythe. Creating a Jack-O-Lantern To create a jack-o-lantern, the caster must be a druid of at least 11th level. Creating a jack-o-lantern involves placing a single pumpkin seed into the mouth of a corpse and burying it in an open field. The body must be that of a humanoid of at least 6th level; the jack-o-lantern has the same HD and alignment as the humanoid did in life. Once the body is buried, the creator must cast control plants, awaken, summon nature's ally IV, and fire seeds-in that order. The pumpkin plant that grows from it must be carefully nurtured and tended, requiring a DC 15 Profession (farmer) check. The farming check doesn't need to be made by the creator. Several pumpkins will grow on the vine, one of which will contain the essence of the nature spirit that will eventually animate the plant. The creator must make a DC 20 Knowledge (nature) check to determine which pumpkin holds the essence-all others must be picked off the vine and discarded. By harvest time, if the creator chose the right pumpkin, the jack-o-lantern will animate and seek out is creator for instructions.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","gear":"rusty pitchfork"},{"name":"Jaguar","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"animal","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +2 natural","hp":26,"hd":"4d8+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":6,"ref":7,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"climb","speed":20},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"2 claw +7 (1d4+4), bite +8 (1d8+4 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"pounce"},{"type":"rake","details":"2 claws +7, 1d4+4"}],"strength":18,"dexterity":17,"constitution":15,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":13,"charisma":6,"baseatk":3,"cmb":7,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":20,"cmd_special":"+4 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Improved Natural Attack (bite)"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":3,"special":"+11 balancing"},{"skill":"Climb","value":16},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11,"special":"+15 in heavy undergrowth and tall grass"},{"skill":"Swim","value":16}],"environments":["warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"The powerful feline's muscles ripple beneath a golden coat covered in dark spots with a smaller markings within those spots.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"The jaguar is a highly aggressive and ferocious great cat that makes its home in dense forests or swampy areas near a source of fresh water.\n\nOf all the big cats, the jaguar is the most at home in the water. They are excellent swimmers and stalk their prey through deep water where other cats won't go. They also excel at climbing and often spend time hunting tree-dwelling animals (such as monkeys).\n\nThe jaguar is highly territorial with its personal hunting ground often covering an area of several square miles. The typical jaguar subsists on a diet of animals such as deer, tapir, elk, fish, armadillos, and other small animals. If hungry or its food supply is thin, jaguars have been known to attack prey much larger than itself. (Creatures as large as warhorses are known to have fallen victim to the powerful jaws of the jaguar.) The jaguar has no rival among the other big cats.\n\nJaguars are solitary creatures, but occasionally a lair is discovered that contains a female and 1d4 cubs. Cubs are often captured and sold on the market where they can be trained and raised.\n\nJaguars are sometimes confused with leopards. Both cats have a brownish-yellow base fur with dark spots or markings. The jaguar can be distinguished by the smaller markings inside the spots. A jaguar's forelimbs and head are slightly larger than the average leopard (another distinguishing characteristic). Black jaguars are often called black panthers (a misnomer applied to black leopards sometimes as well).\n\nJaguars stalk their prey and prefer to attack from ambush, swooping in and felling prey with one swift stroke of their powerful jaws. Jaguars often lock their jaws on its prey, hanging on until their prey is dead. The kill is then dragged to a safe locale and devoured or carried back to the lair where the food is divided among the cubs.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Jupiter Bloodsucker","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"plant","init":-3,"senses":[{"type":"blindsense","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"bloodsense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":7,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"-3 Dex, +8 natural","hp":30,"hd":"4d8+12","hdvalue":4,"fort":7,"ref":-2,"will":1,"immunities":["plant traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to fire"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":5}],"melee":"6 vine leaves +4 (1d6+1 plus blood drain and grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"blood drain","details":"1 Con per vine leaf"},{"type":"smother"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":5,"constitution":16,"wisdom":10,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":4,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":11,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["solitary","patch (2-5)","bed (6-10)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This plant is a man-sized tangle of leaves and roots. Four large dark green and serrated leaves top the brown, red-veined roots.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Bloodsense","kind":"Ex","details":"A Jupiter bloodsucker can detect any living creature that has blood in its body to a range of 60 ft. by scent."},{"type":"Smother","kind":"Ex","details":"By making a successful grapple check (with a +2 bonus for every vine-leaf attached to a foe at the beginning of its turn), a Jupiter bloodsucker can cover a grappled opponent's nose and mouth. An opponent caught in this way must hold its breath or begin suffocating (see Suffocation). A vineleaf attached in this way doesn't drain blood."}],"description":"The Jupiter bloodsucker, or vampire plant, is a small, seemingly ordinary plant. A creature looking closely at the roots may notice that the stems are transparent and that blood seems to course through them (DC 15 Perception check from 5 ft. or less to notice). On the bottom of each leaf are many small, sharp thorns. These are used to attach to a victim and drain its blood. The Jupiter bloodsucker attacks with its leaves in combat, attempting to grapple and pin a foe. A grabbed opponent is drained of blood by the thorny leaves. At the same time, one leaf covers the victim's face, attempting to smother it.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Jupiter Bloodsucker originally appeared in the Basic module B3 Palace of the Silver Princess (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission. It appears in the orange-covered version (the original), not the revised green-covered module."},{"name":"Kathlin","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"NG","size":"Large","type":"magical beast","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +4 natural, -1 size","hp":34,"hd":"4d10+12","hdvalue":4,"fort":7,"ref":6,"will":2,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":50}],"melee":"4 hooves +7 (1d6+4), bite +7 (1d4+4)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":18,"dexterity":15,"constitution":17,"intelligence":5,"wisdom":13,"charisma":8,"baseatk":4,"cmb":9,"cmd":21,"cmd_special":"+8 vs. trip","feats":[{"feat":"Endurance"},{"feat":"Run"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":8}],"languages":["Common","Sylvan (cannot speak)"],"sq":[{"type":"great endurance"}],"environments":["temperate plains"],"organizations":["solitary","herd (4-13)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature resembles a blackish-brown warhorse that rears, exposing six muscular legs and terrible hooves.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Great Endurance","kind":"Ex","details":"A kathlin gains a +4 bonus to Constitution checks made to continue running. This bonus stacks with the bonus to Constitution checks the kathlin gains from the Endurance feat."}],"description":"Kathlins are powerful, six-legged horses that spend their time roaming the temperate plains they call home. They generally avoid contact with civilized races, preferring the seclusion of their homeland above all else. Kathlin congregate in herds and two types of herds can be encountered; bachelor or mixed. A bachelor herd consists of all males. Each male in such a herd is young and hasn't reached full maturity yet. The second type of kathlin herd is a mixed herd that contains both females and males. In a mixed herd there will always be at least one male for every three females (its part of the harem-like structure of the kathlin society). When a bachelor male reaches maturity, it leaves the bachelor herd and seeks out a mixed herd where it selects up to three females to mate with. Should the kathlin select a female that is mated with another male, a battle ensues. The male that wins this fight claims the female as its own. Kathlins can be trained as mounts or beasts of burden. Young are usually easier to train than older kathlins, and males make better mounts than females. They can also be trained to serve as a mount in combat. They are fearless creatures and do quite well in such situations. Trained properly, a kathlin makes an excellent combat steed. When fighting, a kathlin rears back on its hind legs and slashes with its hooves or gnashes at a foe with its bite. Non-domesticated or untrained kathlin back away from combat if overwhelmed. They don't do so out of fear, but are intelligent enough to know when they are beaten. Training a Kathlin A kathlin requires training before it can bear a rider in combat. To be trained, a kathlin must have a friendly attitude toward the trainer (this can be achieved through a successful Diplomacy check). Training a friendly kathlin requires six weeks of work and a successful DC 25 Handle Animal check. Riding a kathlin doesn't require an exotic saddle. A kathlin can fight while carrying a rider, but the rider cannot also attack unless he or she makes a successful Ride check. Kathlin young are worth 5,000 gp on the open market. Professional trainers charge 1,000 gp to rear or train a kathlin. Carrying Capacity: A light load for a kathlin is up to 300 pounds; a medium load, 301 to 600 pounds; and a heavy load, 601 to 900 pounds. A kathlin can drag 4,500 pounds. Paladin's Mount A paladin of 6th level or higher may call a kathlin as his or her special mount.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Kech","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"monstrous humanoid","init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +1 dodge, +4 natural","hp":32,"hd":"5d10+5","hdvalue":5,"fort":2,"ref":6,"will":5,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"2 claws +6 (1d4+1 and bite +6 (1d6+1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rend","details":"2 claws, 1d4+1"}],"strength":13,"dexterity":15,"constitution":12,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":13,"charisma":11,"baseatk":5,"cmb":6,"cmd":19,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Mobility"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":17},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":14,"special":"+20 in forested areas"},{"skill":"Survival","value":9}],"languages":["Kech"],"sq":[{"type":"pass without trace"}],"environments":["any forests"],"organizations":["pack (2-5)","band (2-8 plus 50% noncombatants)"],"treasure":"standard special Abilities Pass Without Trace (Ex) A kech can move across any ground-ice, snow, mud-without leaving any footprints. Tracking a kech by nonmagical means is impossible.","description_visual":"This creature is a monkey-like man-sized humanoid with sapphire blue eyes and greenish-brown leaves covering its body.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"Kechs are monkey-like humanoids standing about 6 ft. tall and weighing about 150 pounds. They make their homes in trees and prefer to move through the trees rather than on the ground. They have an almost human organization and society among the various tribes and clans. Family units dwell in a single lair (usually a hollowed tree or small hut built among the branches of a leafy tree). The kech has leathery skin that on first glance resembles a covering of leaves and foliage. Kechs attack from ambush, preferring to ensnare their prey in pits, traps, or the like. If faced with a weak adversary, they attack with a frontal assault, seeking to kill as quickly as possible. Slain prey is dragged into the kech's lair and devoured at the creatures' leisure.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Knight Gaunt","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":21,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":20,"ac_mods":"+6 armor, +1 Dex, +4 natural","hp":26,"hd":"4d8+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":3,"ref":2,"will":5,"immunities":["undead traits"],"resists":[{"type":"cold","value":10}],"sr":14,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20,"special":"30 ft. without armor"}],"melee":"longsword +6 (1d8+3 plus bleed) or 2 claws +5 (1d4+2 plus bleed)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"bleed","details":"1d4"},{"type":"smite good"},{"type":"weapon attunement"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":12,"intelligence":11,"wisdom":13,"charisma":15,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmd":16,"feats":[{"feat":"Cleave"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Intimidate","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Ride","value":0},{"skill":"Stealth","value":3}],"languages":["Common"],"environments":["any"],"organizations":["solitary","troop (2-8)"],"treasure":"standard (chainmail armor, longsword)","description_visual":"This desiccated creature resembles a once proud knight fallen from grace. Its armor reveals its former station. The creature's eyes burn red and its hands end in wicked claws.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Smite Good","kind":"Su","details":"Once per day, a knight gaunt can smite good. As a swift action, the knight gaunt chooses one target within sight. If this target is good, the knight gaunt adds its Charisma bonus on its attack rolls and adds its Hit Dice on all damage rolls made against that target. If the target is an outsider with the good subtype or a good-aligned creature with levels of cleric of paladin, the bonus to damage on the first successful attack is doubled. Regardless of the target, smite good attacks automatically bypass any DR the creature might possess. Additionally, the knight gaunt gains a deflection bonus equal to its Charisma bonus to AC against attacks made by the target of the smite. The smite good remains in effect until the target of the smite is dead or until 24 hours have passed."},{"type":"Weapon Attunement","kind":"Su","details":"A knight gaunt gains a +1 bonus on attack rolls and damage rolls with any melee weapon it wields. Additionally, it gains the bleed special attack with any melee weapon it wields."}],"description":"A knight gaunt is an undead creature created when a paladin falls in battle. Other than a few fleeting thoughts perhaps of the martial training they received in life, these creatures have no memories of their former lives. A knight gaunt often wears the armor it wore in life and wields the weapons it carried into its last battle before rising as undead.\n\nKnight gaunts are most often found in the employ of death knights serving as companions or knights-in-training. Others wander the world seeking out goodness wherever they find it, squashing it beneath their steel-booted heel and putting the righteous to their blades. Paladins and clerics of good alignment are hated by many knight gaunts for they represent everything the knight gaunt isn't, and perhaps once was or once strived to be.\n\nA typical knight gaunt stands 6 ft. tall and weighs 180 pounds. It wears the armor it wore when alive and still carries the weapons it wielded in life. Its skin is ash gray in color and its eyes are milky white with fiery red pupils.\n\nA knight gaunt fights with bladed weapons in battle, preferring longswords, greatswords, and axes to all others. Only when disarmed does it resort to using its claws. A knight gaunt that retains its mount (that is undead now as well) or procures some undead steed to carry it into battle wields a lance in addition to its other weapons. In battle, a knight gaunt is relentless, attacking with its blades until all of its foes are vanquished.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Large Acid Quasi-elemental","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"outsider","subtypes":["acid","elemental","extraplanar"],"init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["fumes (5 ft., Fort DC 16)"],"ac":19,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +8 natural, -1 size","hp":51,"hd":"6d10+18","hdvalue":6,"fort":5,"ref":7,"will":5,"dr":"5/-","immunities":["acid","elemental traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to water"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"swim","speed":80}],"melee":"2 slams +8 (1d6+3 plus 1d6 acid)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"acid"},{"type":"drench"}],"strength":17,"dexterity":14,"constitution":17,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":11,"charisma":11,"baseatk":6,"cmb":10,"cmd":22,"feats":[{"feat":"Cleave"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Knowledge (planes)","value":6},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":7},{"skill":"Swim","value":11}],"languages":["Aquan"],"environments":["any (Quasi-Plane of Acid)"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"What was once a still puddle of green liquid rises up like a wave, lashing out with acid-dripping tendrils. In the midst of the pool, one can barely make out regions of darker green that form a vaguely human face.","group":"Quasi-elemental","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Acid","kind":"Ex","details":"Acid quasi-elementals are living creatures of acid; any melee hit deals acid damage. Creatures hitting an acid quasi-elemental unarmed or with natural attacks take acid damage as though hit by the quasi-elemental's slam attack."},{"type":"Acid Swimmer","kind":"Ex","details":"Acid quasi-elementals cannot swim in water. The swim speed given in the statistics block is their movement only through acid pools or the acidic nature of their environment on their home plane."},{"type":"Drench","kind":"Ex","details":"The quasi-elemental's touch puts out torches, campfires, exposed lanterns, and other open flames of nonmagical origin if these are of Large size or smaller. The creature can dispel magical fire as dispel magic (caster level equals the quasi-elemental's HD)."},{"type":"Fumes","kind":"Ex","details":"The fumes from an acid quasi-elemental's body act as an inhaled poison. Creatures within 5 ft. of an acid quasi-elemental must make succeed on a Fortitude save or take 1 point of Constitution damage each round. This poison doesn't have a frequency; a creature is safe as soon as it moves more than 5 ft. away from the acid quasi-elemental."},{"type":"Vulnerability to Water","kind":"Ex","details":"An acid elemental erupts in a violent chemical reaction when touched by water. An acid quasi-elemental takes 1d4 points of fire damage for each gallon of water poured into it. The reaction is so violent that all creatures within 5 ft. of the acid quasi-elemental when the water hits it must succeed on a DC 15 Reflex save or take the same damage. Pouring water onto an acid quasi-elemental requires a successful ranged touch attack. Water from other sources, such as spells or effects that create water but don't specify an amount in gallons, deal 1d4 points of fire damage per caster level to an acid quasi-elemental within its area of effect. Damage from water-based attacks, like the slam attack of a water elemental, is increased by 50% against an acid quasi-elemental. Note that this reaction doesn't occur when the acid quasi-elemental touches water, only when water touches the acid quasi-elemental."}],"description":"Nestled among the various elemental there lies a plane composed entirely of acid. It is a place of noxious fumes and roiling, bubbling, pools. Plane jumpers do well to avoid this place as it is considered by many to be one of the deadliest, if not the deadliest, of the elemental-based planes. Acid quasi-elementals rarely journey from their native plane, except when summoned. They don't like the Material Plane and when called to the place, are usually angered and always a bit uncomfortable. They have no trouble moving on land, but prefer the sanctity of their native plane to all others. At rest, an acid quasi-elemental is a clear puddle of liquid with a slightly green hue. It can rise up like a wave in a manner similar to a water elemental, or ooze along solid surfaces by sending out tendrils and pulling itself along. Wherever it travels, it leaves behind a shallow trough that emits wisps of smoke. Acid quasi-elementals are almost always encountered in large pools of acid when on the Material Plane and rarely leave these pools when combating foes. A favored tactic is to grab a foe and pull it into the quasi-elemental's acid pool, subjecting it to massive amounts of acid damage.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Large Monstrous Sea Wasp","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"vermin","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"watersense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":12,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +2 natural, -1 size","hp":22,"hd":"4d8+4","hdvalue":4,"fort":5,"ref":2,"will":1,"defensive_abilities":["transparent"],"immunities":["vermin traits"],"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":60}],"melee":"tentacle +3 (1d8+1 plus poison)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"entangle"}],"strength":13,"dexterity":13,"constitution":12,"wisdom":10,"charisma":2,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmd":16,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","skills":[{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Stealth","value":7},{"skill":"Swim","value":9}],"sq":[{"type":"jet"},{"type":"transparent"}],"environments":["warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","ring (2-5)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"A translucent blue and nearly invisible in water, this cube-shaped jellyfish has four distinct sides and trails dozens of extremely long tentacles.","group":"Sea Wasp","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Entangle","kind":"Ex","details":"A sea wasp can entangle a creature up to two sizes smaller than itself with its tentacles with a successful CMB check. An entangled opponent suffers tentacle damage and the effect of its poison each round the hold is maintained. An entangled creature still receives a save to avoid the effects of its poison. While a foe is entangled, the sea wasp cannot attack any other creature."},{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Sting-injury; the effects of the poison are determined by the creature's size. The DC of the save is Constitution-based. Size Fort DC Frequency Effect Cure Tiny 10 1/round for 1d4 rounds 1 Con 1 save Small 11 1/round for 1d4 rounds 1d2 Con 1 save Medium 11 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d3 Con 1 save Large 13 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d4 Con 2 consecutive saves Huge 15 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d6 Con 2 consecutive saves Gargantuan 20 1/round for 1d6 rounds 1d8 Con 3 consecutive saves"},{"type":"Jet","kind":"Ex","details":"A monstrous sea wasp can jet backward once per round as a full-round action at a speed equal to four times its swim speed. It must move in a straight line, but doesn't provoke attacks of opportunity while jetting."},{"type":"Transparent","kind":"Ex","details":"A monstrous sea wasp is nearly invisible floating in the water. An observer must make a successful DC 20 Perception check to notice the sea wasp."},{"type":"Watersense","kind":"Ex","details":"Monstrous sea wasps can automatically sense the location of anything within 60 ft. that is in contact with water."}],"description":"Monstrous sea wasps are deadly aquatic creatures with long, writhing and highly poisonous tentacles that extend for several yards. They inhabit warm waters and tend to congregate near coastal regions or river mouths where they wait for prey to wash downstream to them. Monstrous sea wasps are also called box jellyfish or marine stingers. Almost invisible in the water, the sea wasp has four eyes, one on each side of the bell, connected to a nerve ring inside the main body. They have no brain, but are able to process visual information. These eyes are invisible to all but the keenest observers. Monstrous sea wasps feed on a variety of marine life including fish, manta rays and stingrays, and crustaceans. The larger monstrous sea wasps also feed on sharks, whales, giant fish, giant squids, octopi, and swimmers. Prey that becomes entangled in its mass of tentacles is quickly stung by the sea wasp and then coiled into its mouth and devoured. Monstrous sea wasps are non-aggressive and don't actively hunt for food. They simply float along in the water waiting for a living creature to swim into its mass of tentacles. When it detects prey in its tentacles, it folds them around the prey, and stings it. A monstrous sea wasp's poison (particularly the larger ones) is meant to instantly kill its prey so it doesn't struggle and damage the sea wasp's delicate form. Sages catalogue the sea wasp's sting as being one of the deadliest and most painful one can experience. A creature stung by a sea wasp that lives to tell about, never forgets its brush with this monstrosity.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Large Obsidian Quasi-elemental","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"outsider","subtypes":["earth","elemental","extraplanar"],"init":-1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":8,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"-1 Dex, +9 natural, -1 size","hp":51,"hd":"6d10+18","hdvalue":6,"fort":5,"ref":4,"will":5,"dr":"5/-","immunities":["elemental traits"],"resists":[{"type":"cold","value":10},{"type":"fire","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"2 claws +11 (1d8+6)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"death throes"},{"type":"molten glass","details":"2d6 fire, DC 16"}],"strength":22,"dexterity":8,"constitution":17,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":11,"charisma":11,"baseatk":6,"cmb":13,"cmd":22,"feats":[{"feat":"Cleave"},{"feat":"Great Cleave"},{"feat":"Improved Natural Attack","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Power Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Knowledge (planes)","value":7},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":4}],"languages":["Terran"],"environments":["any (Quasi-Plane of Obsidian)"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature is a powerfully built, roughly humanoid-shaped monolith of black stone. Its hands end in wicked, serrated and jagged claws. No facial features can be discerned.","group":"Quasi-elemental","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Death Throes","kind":"Ex","details":"When reduced to 0 or less hit points, an obsidian quasi-elemental explodes in a cloud of razor-sharp bits of obsidian. A creature caught in the area can make a Reflex save for half damage. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Molten Glass","kind":"Ex","details":"A successful hit against an obsidian quasi-elemental with a piercing or slashing weapon (or piercing or slashing natural attack) that deals damage equal to its current HD opens a wound that spurts molten glass. The wound bleeds for 1d4 rounds and any creature within 5 ft. must make a Reflex save or take fire damage from the splash. A new save must be made each round the wound bleeds and a creature is within the area. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"In a quasi-realm where elemental water and elemental fire conjoin, there exists a pocket plane known as the Quasi-plane of Obsidian; a plane of barren wastes and blackened rock, of razor-sharp obelisks and fields of sharpened glass. Creatures native to this plane appear as blackened obsidian with jagged, serrated, or clear-cut angles and edges. Some have barbed spikes adorning their elemental forms. Obsidian quasi-elementals are usually encountered on their home plane or on the Plane of Fire. Still some can be found on the Obsidian Plain in the Plane of Molten Skies (see the City of Brass by Necromancer Games). Most, however, prefer the serenity that the Quasi-plane of Obsidian offers, and thus they rarely venture forth unless summoned. Obsidian quasi-elementals are deadly combatants and strike with their sharpened claws, ripping and tearing at their opponents. They are relentless in their attacks and never give quarter.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""}];
